---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Stains",
    "url": "/best-deck-stain-reviews/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Paint",
    "url": "/best-deck-paint/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Redwood Sealer",
    "url": "/best-redwood-sealer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Fence Stain",
    "url": "/best-fence-stain/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Deck Stain Drying Time",
    "url": "/deck-stain-drying-time/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "Best Deck Sealants: 6 Clear Finishes That Keep Boards Dry";
const description =
  "Six clear and lightly tinted deck sealants compared on penetration, UV defense and recoat interval, plus how to tell when a sealant is the wrong product.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "twp-1500-clear",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Oil-based penetrating sealer",
    "name": "TWP 1500 Series Wood Preservative",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "bottomLine": "The best all-round choice for owners who want a penetrating oil that fades gracefully and recoats without drama.",
    "pros": [
      "Penetrating oil formula with no surface film to peel",
      "Labeled as a wood preservative with mildew resistance",
      "Clear and lightly toned options from the same line",
      "Recoats with cleaning only, no stripping",
      "Long track record with deck restoration contractors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not available in every state because of VOC rules",
      "Usually ordered online or from specialty dealers",
      "Longer dry time than water-based options"
    ],
    "description": "TWP, short for Total Wood Preservative, is a long-running oil-based line that contractors often reach for on cedar and pressure-treated decks. The 1500 Series soaks in rather than building a coating, so when it wears it simply looks drier and lighter instead of flaking. That behavior is the main reason it ranks first here: maintenance is a cleaning and a fresh coat, with no stripping between cycles. The line includes a clear version and several lightly toned ones, and the toned versions carry more pigment and generally hold up longer in sun. The label describes mildew and water resistance, and the brand positions it as a preservative rather than a cosmetic finish. Expect the wood to warm a shade even in the clearest version. Drawbacks are practical rather than performance related. The formula is restricted in some low-VOC states, it is harder to buy in person than big-box brands, and it needs warm, dry days to cure well. Prices were roughly 45 to 60 dollars per gallon at the time of writing, less in five-gallon pails."
  },
  {
    "id": "ready-seal-natural",
    "badge": "Easiest to Apply",
    "category": "Oil-based, lightly tinted",
    "name": "Ready Seal Exterior Wood Stain and Sealer, Natural Cedar",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "bottomLine": "The easiest product on this list to apply evenly, and a better long-term choice than a pure clear for most decks.",
    "pros": [
      "Marketed as no back-brushing and no lap marks",
      "Can be sprayed, rolled or brushed",
      "Light tint adds UV protection while keeping grain visible",
      "Forgiving for first-time DIYers",
      "Widely sold in one- and five-gallon sizes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Color develops over about two weeks, so the first look is misleading",
      "Oil-based cleanup and longer dry time",
      "Not a true clear"
    ],
    "description": "Ready Seal built its reputation on being hard to mess up. The manufacturer says the formula does not need back-brushing and will not leave lap marks, and owner feedback largely backs up that claim: people who have struggled with streaky finishes often say Ready Seal was the first product that looked even. It is an oil-based penetrating finish that can go on with a pump sprayer, roller or brush, which makes large decks and long railings quicker. The Natural Cedar shade is about as light as the line goes, so it reads as a warm, nearly clear finish rather than a stain. That small dose of pigment is a practical advantage, because it blocks more UV than a true clear and slows graying. Two things trip people up. The color looks light when wet and deepens over roughly two weeks as it cures, so judge the result after that window. It also needs warm, dry weather and mineral spirits for cleanup. Prices ran roughly 40 to 55 dollars per gallon at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "defy-extreme-clear",
    "badge": "Best Water-Based",
    "category": "Water-based penetrating sealer",
    "name": "DEFY Extreme Clear Wood Sealer",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "bottomLine": "The clear sealer to buy if you want a water-based product that still makes a serious attempt at UV protection.",
    "pros": [
      "Soap-and-water cleanup and low odor",
      "Zinc oxide particles for better UV resistance than typical clears",
      "Fast dry time suits short weather windows",
      "Sold in low-VOC states where many oils are not",
      "Penetrates rather than building a heavy film"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs warm, dry conditions and no dew while drying",
      "Can look flatter than an oil finish",
      "Shorter life on sun-baked horizontal surfaces"
    ],
    "description": "DEFY Extreme Clear is a water-based penetrating sealer that tries to close the usual gap between clear finishes and real sun protection. Its trick is zinc oxide nanoparticles, which scatter UV without adding the color that pigments would. That does not make it a semi-transparent stain, but it gives the clear version a better chance of holding natural color than older wax-based repellents. Because it is water-based, cleanup is soap and water, odor is mild, and the product can be shipped to states with strict VOC limits. It dries fast, which helps when a forecast only gives you one good afternoon. The tradeoffs are the usual water-based ones. Apply it on a day that stays warm and dry into the evening; dew or a sudden shower before it sets will leave a blotchy finish. Owners also note that it looks a little flatter than an oil, without the warm, wet look oil gives cedar. Expect yearly recoats on a sunny deck and longer intervals in shade. Prices were roughly 40 to 55 dollars per gallon at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "thompson-water-seal",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "category": "Clear water repellent",
    "name": "Thompson's WaterSeal Advanced Natural Wood Protector",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "bottomLine": "A fine, cheap water repellent for owners who accept yearly recoats and do not mind the wood weathering in color.",
    "pros": [
      "Sold at nearly every hardware store and home center",
      "Low price per gallon",
      "Easy to apply with a pump sprayer or roller",
      "Strong immediate water beading",
      "Good for fences, sheds and outbuildings too"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Very little UV protection; wood still grays",
      "Plan on recoating roughly every year on decks",
      "Stacking coats too soon leaves a tacky surface"
    ],
    "description": "Thompson's WaterSeal is the clear sealant most people have heard of, and the Advanced Natural Wood Protector is the brand's current mainstream clear. Its strength is accessibility: almost every hardware store stocks it, it costs less than most competitors, and it beads water impressively on the day it goes on. For a fence, shed, playset or a deck you plan to refinish with color later, that is often enough. Where it falls short is longevity on open horizontal surfaces. With no pigment and limited UV defense, the wood underneath continues to gray, and the water beading on high-traffic boards typically fades within a year. Many owners are happy with it on that understanding and simply recoat each spring. The common complaint, a sticky or shiny surface, usually comes from applying a second coat while the first is still repelling water. Do the water drop test before each recoat and apply only when the wood absorbs. Prices were roughly 20 to 35 dollars per gallon at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "olympic-maximum-sealant",
    "badge": "Best Big-Box Clear",
    "category": "Water-based clear sealant",
    "name": "Olympic Maximum Waterproofing Sealant, Clear",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "bottomLine": "A solid, easy-to-find clear option when you want a step up from basic water repellents without ordering online.",
    "pros": [
      "Widely stocked at Lowe's",
      "Water-based, with easy cleanup",
      "Mildew-resistant coating per the label",
      "Also sold in tinted versions in the same line",
      "Reasonable price per gallon"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Clear version still lets wood gray over time",
      "Can build a light film with repeated coats",
      "Best results need careful prep"
    ],
    "description": "Olympic Maximum is Lowe's flagship deck finish family, and the clear waterproofing sealant is the no-color member of it. It is water-based, so it cleans up with soap and water and suits owners who would rather not handle solvent. The label calls out water repellency and resistance to mildew on the coating, and the broader Maximum line includes toned, semi-transparent and solid versions if you decide later that you want more color and UV protection. For a homeowner who shops locally, that range makes it easy to step up without switching systems. Like every clear on this list, it does not stop sun from graying the wood. It also behaves a little more like a coating than a pure penetrating oil, so heavy layering over many years can leave a surface that eventually needs a stripper rather than just a cleaner. Apply thin coats, back-brush, and recoat only when the water test says the wood is absorbing again. Prices were roughly 30 to 45 dollars per gallon at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "seal-once-marine",
    "badge": "Best Near Water",
    "category": "Water-based penetrating sealer",
    "name": "Seal-Once Marine Premium Wood Sealer",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "bottomLine": "The pick for docks, boardwalks and decks next to ponds or pools, where a lower-solvent penetrating sealer makes sense.",
    "pros": [
      "Water-based penetrating polymer formula",
      "Marketed for docks, boathouses and marine wood",
      "Low odor and easy cleanup",
      "Clear and tinted versions available",
      "Does not form a thick film"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Harder to find in stores than mass-market brands",
      "Higher price than basic repellents",
      "Clear version offers limited UV defense"
    ],
    "description": "Seal-Once Marine is a water-based penetrating sealer sold for docks, piers and wood that sits near water, which makes it a sensible choice for decks beside a pond, lake or pool. It soaks into the wood rather than forming a thick film, and the brand emphasizes its lower solvent content compared with traditional oil sealers. That matters when runoff or overspray will reach water or planted areas you care about. Owners generally describe it as easy to spray or roll, with quick drying and soap-and-water cleanup. The clear version shares the limits of all clears: it keeps water out but does little to keep sun from graying the surface, and the line includes tinted options for anyone who wants more UV defense. It costs more than basic repellents and is more often bought online or from marine suppliers than at a big-box store. If your deck sits in open sun far from any water, a toned oil may be a better value; near the shoreline, this is the one to look at first. Prices ran roughly 45 to 65 dollars per gallon at the time of writing."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Deck Sealants",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-deck-sealant/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Sealant versus stain: which one does what?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A deck sealant is mainly a water repellent and is usually clear or very lightly tinted, so the wood keeps its natural look. A stain contains pigment, which adds color and blocks UV light much more effectively. Many products are sold as stain and sealer in one and sit between the two. The more pigment a finish carries, the longer it generally lasts in sun."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a clear deck sealant last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On an open, sunny deck most clear sealants keep beading water for about one to two years on the floor boards, and somewhat longer on railings and vertical surfaces. Shade, climate and foot traffic shift that range a lot. Lightly tinted versions of the same products typically last longer. The water drop test is the best way to know when your deck needs another coat."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can sealant go on top of an old stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can apply a penetrating sealant over an old penetrating stain once it has worn enough that water soaks in, after cleaning the deck. You cannot put a penetrating sealant over solid stain, paint or a peeling film, because it has nothing to soak into. Those finishes need stripping first, or a product designed to go over them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is oil-based or water-based deck sealant better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither wins outright. Oil-based penetrating sealers tend to soak deeper, warm the wood's color and fade gradually, which makes recoating easy. Water-based sealers dry faster, clean up with soap and water, have less odor and are legal in low-VOC states. Choose oil for dense or weathered wood in moderate weather, and water-based for short weather windows or tighter VOC rules."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I seal a deck in the fall or spring?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Either works if the weather fits the label. Most products want air and surface temperatures between roughly 50 and 90 degrees, dry wood, and a rain-free day or two afterward. Fall often offers stable dry weather and cooler sun, which helps penetration. Spring sealing protects the deck before summer heat. Avoid days when dew or frost will settle before the finish dries."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do composite decks need sealant?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Composite and PVC decking are not designed to absorb a wood sealant, and most manufacturers advise against coating the boards. Applying one can leave a slippery or blotchy film and may affect the warranty. Clean composite decks with a cleaner the board maker approves. Any wood framing underneath, such as stair stringers or fascia, can still be treated."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many coats of deck sealant should I apply?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most penetrating sealants call for one coat, and some ask for a second coat applied wet on wet before the first dries. Extra coats beyond what the wood can absorb only sit on the surface and turn tacky. Very dense hardwoods may only take one thin coat, while rough or weathered pine may take more. Always follow the product label."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Deck & Wood Finishes",
    tags: [
      "best deck sealant",
      "clear deck sealer",
      "deck waterproofing",
      "water repellent for wood",
      "deck maintenance",
      "penetrating wood sealer"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The 6 Best Deck Sealants for Clear, Dry Boards
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          A clear sealant keeps water out of a deck and leaves the grain alone. It does very little about sun. These six are ranked on what the labels and data sheets promise, how owners say they age, and which deck each one actually suits.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">12 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>Top Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#sealant-or-stain" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Sealant or Stain?</span></a>
              <a href="#how-sealants-work" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>How Sealants Work</span></a>
              <a href="#buying-factors" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>What to Compare</span></a>
              <a href="#by-wood" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Matching the Wood</span></a>
              <a href="#prep" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Prep That Matters</span></a>
              <a href="#application" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Applying It Right</span></a>
              <a href="#coverage-cost" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Coverage and Cost</span></a>
              <a href="#recoat" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Recoat Schedule</span></a>
              <a href="#end-grain" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Cut Ends and Hardware</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people who buy a deck sealant want two things at once: a deck that looks like bare wood and a deck that never turns gray. A clear product can deliver the first and only slow down the second. Water repellency is the easy part of the job, and nearly every product on this list does it well for a season or two. Ultraviolet light is what grays, checks and erodes the surface, and clear finishes have almost no pigment to block it. So the honest buying question is not which sealant lasts five years, because none of them do on a sunny deck. It is which one goes on cleanly, beads water longest for your climate, and is easy enough to recoat that you will actually do it every year or two.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Deck Sealants, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rankings draw on manufacturer data sheets, label coverage and recoat guidance, VOC and availability limits, and recurring patterns in owner feedback. We did not run our own weathering trials. Each pick is matched to the deck and the owner it fits best rather than to a single scoreboard.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="sealant-or-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sealant or Stain? Decide Before You Shop
          </h2>

          <p>
            The words sealant, sealer, water repellent and stain get used interchangeably on store shelves, which is why so many decks get the wrong product. For this guide, a deck sealant is a clear or very lightly tinted finish whose main job is keeping liquid water out of the wood. A stain carries enough pigment to change the color and block a meaningful share of sunlight. Plenty of cans are labeled stain and sealer in one, and those sit on a sliding scale between the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick a clear sealant when the wood is new or freshly cleaned, you like its natural color, and you are willing to recoat on a short cycle. Cedar, redwood and tropical hardwoods are the usual candidates because their color is the reason people bought them. A clear coat on those species keeps them from soaking up rain, swelling and cupping, while letting the grain show exactly as it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick a tinted or semi-transparent stain instead when the deck sits in full afternoon sun, when gray weathering bothers you, or when you want three years between projects instead of one. Pigment is the only thing in a finish that blocks UV in a lasting way. Our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a> cover that end of the scale, and they are the better read if your deck faces south or west with no shade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick neither when the boards are badly weathered, split or covered in old failing film. A clear finish will not hide anything, and penetrating products cannot get through peeling paint or solid stain. Those decks either need stripping back to bare wood or a different approach entirely, which our guide to <a href="/best-deck-paint/">deck paints and resurfacers</a> walks through by condition.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Clear sealant protects against water, pigment protects against sun.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you want grain-perfect color and accept yearly upkeep, buy a sealant. If you want fewer weekends spent on the deck, buy something with color in it. Most disappointment with clear products comes from expecting stain-level life from a product that carries no pigment.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-sealants-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Deck Sealants Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear deck sealants fall into three chemistry families. The oldest is the paraffin wax water repellent: a thin carrier loaded with wax that lodges in the surface pores and makes water bead. It is cheap, fast and effective for a while, but wax sits near the surface and wears off quickly under foot traffic and sun. Many budget clear products still rely on some version of this approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second family is the penetrating oil. These use drying oils such as linseed or soy alongside paraffinic oils, thinned so they soak into the top fraction of an inch. They harden inside the wood rather than on top, so there is no film to peel. When they wear, they fade and dry out rather than flake, which makes the next coat simple: clean and reapply. Oil finishes tend to darken and warm the wood a shade, even when labeled clear.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is the water-based penetrating sealer, built on acrylic or other polymer resins that are small enough to enter the wood. Modern versions clean up with soap and water, carry far less solvent, and can be shipped to every state. Some add zinc oxide or transparent iron oxide particles to improve UV resistance without adding visible color. They usually dry to the touch in an hour or two, which helps when weather windows are short.
          </p>

          <p>
            What none of these families can do in clear form is stop UV from breaking down lignin, the natural glue that holds wood fibers together. Once lignin degrades, the surface fibers loosen, turn gray and wash away a little with every rain. A clear sealant slows the water damage that follows and keeps mildew from taking hold, but the color still drifts. Additives such as UV absorbers buy time; they do not change the basic tradeoff.
          </p>

