        <div id="buying-factors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What to Compare on the Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Penetration versus film.</strong> For horizontal decking, a penetrating product almost always beats one that builds a surface film. Deck boards flex, expand with moisture and take abrasion from shoes, chairs and grills. A film cracks along those stresses and lets water in behind it, which then lifts more film. A penetrating finish has nothing to crack, so failure looks like gradual fading instead of patchy peeling.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mildewcide.</strong> Shaded decks and humid climates grow mildew on almost any finish, and oil-based products give it food. Look for labels that list a mildewcide or describe the product as a wood preservative. That matters more on a deck under trees in Georgia than on one in open sun in Colorado, but it is rarely a reason to avoid a product that otherwise fits.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Application window.</strong> Every label lists a temperature range, usually around 40 or 50 degrees up to the low 90s, and a rain-free period after application. Water-based products generally want warmer, drier conditions and fail if dew settles before they dry. Oil-based products tolerate cooler days but stay tacky longer. Read this before buying; a product you cannot apply in your usual season is the wrong product.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>VOC limits and availability.</strong> Several states and air districts cap the solvent content of wood coatings. Some oil-based sealants are reformulated, sold in a different version, or not shipped at all to those areas. Check the manufacturer's state list before you plan a project around a specific brand, especially if you live in California, the Northeast or the Mid-Atlantic.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Recoat method.</strong> The best sealant for a homeowner is the one that can be recoated without stripping. Most penetrating products only need a cleaning before the next coat. A few water-based clear coats build a light film over several applications and eventually need sanding or stripping. The label or data sheet usually says how to maintain the finish; read it as carefully as the coverage figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-wood" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Matching the Sealant to Your Decking
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Pressure-treated pine.</strong> This is most American decking, and it is the species that benefits least from a clear finish because its color is not the attraction. Treated pine also arrives wet from the treatment process and will not accept any finish until it dries. If you are building new, read our guide on whether and when <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">you can stain pressure-treated wood</a> before you buy anything. Once dry, a lightly tinted penetrating oil or water-based sealer is usually a better value than a true clear.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cedar.</strong> Western red cedar is soft, absorbent and full of natural oils and extractives. Penetrating oils suit it well, and toned clears that add a hint of cedar color hold the look longer than a pure clear. Avoid aggressive pressure washing on cedar before sealing, because its soft earlywood erodes quickly and leaves a fuzzy, uneven surface that absorbs finish unevenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Redwood.</strong> Redwood behaves like cedar but with more tannin and a stronger natural color that bleaches fast in sun. Clear sealers keep redwood dry but rarely keep it red. If preserving that color is the goal, a redwood-toned penetrating finish is the realistic answer, and our <a href="/best-redwood-sealer/">redwood sealer guide</a> covers the options in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tropical hardwoods.</strong> Ipe, cumaru, garapa and similar species are so dense that many standard sealants sit on the surface and turn sticky. They need finishes formulated for hardwood, applied thinly, with every drop of excess wiped off. Many owners simply let these woods weather to silver and clean them once a year, which is a legitimate choice that costs nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Composite and PVC decking.</strong> Do not seal composite or PVC boards with any of the products here. They are not designed to absorb a penetrating finish, and most composite manufacturers advise against coatings except for specific cleaners. If a composite deck looks dull, clean it with a product the board maker approves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Prep: Where Most Sealant Jobs Succeed or Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealant can only soak into wood that is clean, open and dry. Gray surface fibers, mildew, pollen and old finish all block penetration. The simplest preparation on a weathered deck is a two-step process: a sodium percarbonate or other oxygen-based deck cleaner to lift dirt and mildew, followed by an oxalic acid brightener that neutralizes the cleaner and restores the wood's color. Skip chlorine bleach; it kills mildew but damages wood fibers and leaves the surface blotchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pressure washer speeds cleaning but is the most common way to damage a deck. Keep the pressure low, use a wide fan tip, hold the wand a foot or more from the surface, and move with the grain. The goal is rinsing loosened dirt, not carving the board. A rotary attachment helps keep the spray even across wide areas; our picks for the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners</a> include models that work on wood at reduced pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            After washing, the wood needs to dry. Most sealant labels ask for at least 24 to 48 hours of dry weather, and some want the moisture content below a specific figure. An inexpensive pin moisture meter takes the guesswork out; readings in the low to mid teens are generally ready. Without a meter, sprinkle water on the boards: if it soaks in within a few minutes, the wood will take finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            If there is an old film-forming finish on the deck, deal with it now. Clear penetrating sealants cannot bond through peeling paint or solid stain. Use a chemical stripper made for decks, then the brightener, then let it dry. Sanding with a coarse grit also works on small areas, but sand no finer than about 60 to 80 grit on decking, or the surface becomes too closed to absorb finish evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, protect what you do not want coated. Cover plants with breathable drop cloths, tape off house siding where the deck meets it, and remove furniture. Oil-based sealants on brick or concrete leave dark stains that are hard to remove, so plan where the overspray and drips will land before you open the can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Applying a Sealant Without Lap Marks or Sticky Spots
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work in shade or on an overcast day. Direct sun dries the surface too fast, which causes lap marks with water-based products and keeps oil from penetrating before the solvent flashes off. Early morning after the dew has dried, or late afternoon on the shaded side of the house, gives the most working time. Check the forecast for at least a full day without rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coat two or three boards at a time along their full length. Stopping in the middle of a board leaves a visible line where wet finish meets dried finish. A pump sprayer or roller moves product fast, but follow each section with a brush or pad to push finish into the grain and spread puddles. Back-brushing is the single habit that most improves how a sealant looks and lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not flood the surface. More product is not more protection with a penetrating finish; anything the wood cannot absorb stays on top, dries tacky and collects dirt. On dense or new wood, wipe up what is still pooled after the label's wait time, usually 15 to 30 minutes. Many sealants call for a single coat, while others want a second coat applied wet on wet, before the first dries. Follow the label exactly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do railings and vertical surfaces first, then the deck boards, working toward the exit so you do not trap yourself. Finish the ends of each board and the edges along the house. Stairs come last because they are the path off the deck. Allow at least the label's foot traffic time, and longer for furniture, which can mark a finish that is dry to the touch but not cured.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean up immediately. Oily rags used with linseed and other drying oils can heat as they cure and catch fire. Lay them flat outdoors to dry, or soak them in water in a sealed metal container, before disposal. This is not optional caution; it is a well-documented cause of house fires.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Oily rags can ignite on their own.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rags, pads and paper towels soaked with drying oils generate heat as they cure. Never ball them up in a trash can or leave them in a pile. Spread them flat outside to dry or store them under water in a closed metal can until disposal.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Coverage and Cost: How Much to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure the deck surface, then add railings and stairs separately. Railings are surprisingly large: a standard balustrade with pickets often adds 40 to 60 percent to the square footage of the floor alone because every picket has four faces. Stairs add their treads, risers and stringers. Undercounting railings is the main reason people run short mid-project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage figures on labels assume smooth wood. Rough-sawn, weathered or freshly stripped boards absorb far more. A typical penetrating sealant lists 150 to 300 square feet per gallon on smooth surfaces and as little as 100 to 150 on rough or porous ones. Plan on the low end for a first application on an old deck and on the high end for a maintenance coat on a deck that was sealed recently.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">150-300</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, smooth wood</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100-150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, rough or weathered</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$25-$60</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per gallon at the time of writing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-2 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usual clear-coat life on open decks</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Prices for the products on this list ranged roughly from 25 to 60 dollars per gallon at the time of writing, with five-gallon pails cutting the unit price noticeably. For a 300 square foot deck with railings and stairs, a homeowner typically needs two to three gallons of sealant for a first coat, plus cleaner and brightener at another 30 to 60 dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring out adds labor, and that is where the real cost sits. Professional deck cleaning and sealing is commonly priced per square foot and varies widely by region. Because clear sealants need frequent recoating, the lifetime cost of a cheap clear product can exceed that of a tinted finish that lasts three times as long. Compare cost per year of protection, not price per can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Know When to Recoat
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most reliable test is the water drop. Sprinkle a little water on the boards in the areas that get the most sun and foot traffic. If it beads up and sits there, the sealant is still working. If it darkens the wood and soaks in within a minute or so, it is time to clean and recoat. Check in late spring and again in early fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect sunny, high-traffic areas to fail first: the path from the door to the grill, the steps and the boards right in front of a south-facing railing. Shaded sections may still repel water years later. You can recoat only the worn areas with most penetrating oils, because they blend; water-based products are less forgiving and usually look best when the whole surface is redone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not stack coats on a deck that still beads water. Fresh sealant cannot soak into wood that is still repelling it, so the new coat sits on top and turns sticky. This is the most common complaint owners report about clear products, and it is almost always a timing problem rather than a product defect.
          </p>

          <p>
            On each recoat, the preparation is lighter than the first time. A deck cleaner, a rinse, a brightener if the wood has darkened and a day or two of drying is usually enough. If you find yourself needing a stripper every time, switch to a different product type, since a well-chosen penetrating sealant should never need stripping between ordinary maintenance coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical rhythm for most clear sealants is a fresh coat every 12 to 24 months on horizontal surfaces and every two to three years on railings and vertical faces. If that pace feels like too much work, move up to a semi-transparent product. Our list of the <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">best exterior wood sealers</a> includes longer-lived options for siding, fences and outdoor furniture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="end-grain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Cut Ends, Fasteners and the Parts Sealant Misses
          </h2>

          <p>
            End grain absorbs water many times faster than the face of a board, and it is where rot and checking usually start. When you build or repair a deck, treat every field cut on pressure-treated lumber with a brush-on end-cut preservative, commonly a copper naphthenate product. Industry treating standards call for field-cut ends to be treated; the factory preservative rarely reaches the core of a thick post or joist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wax emulsion end sealers, such as the products sawmills and woodturners use on green lumber, are designed to slow drying and prevent splits in logs and blanks. They are useful on firewood, turning stock or fresh timbers, but they are not preservatives and should not stand in for an end-cut treatment on structural deck framing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fasteners matter too. Treated lumber is corrosive to plain steel, so framing hardware and screws need to be hot-dip galvanized or stainless to the level the connector maker specifies. A sealant does not protect a corroding joist hanger. If you see rust streaks bleeding from screws, check the fastener type before blaming the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look under the deck as well. Ledger boards, joist tops and beams stay wet longest because water runs between boards and pools on framing. Joist tape on new builds keeps water off the tops of joists and out of screw holes. Sealant applied to the visible surface does nothing for framing that never dries, so good ventilation and flashing at the ledger are part of keeping the whole deck sound.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep screw heads and small gaps tidy. Popped nails and split board ends trap water; sink or replace fasteners and fill splits on railings with an exterior-rated product before sealing. Our guide to the <a href="/best-wood-filler/">best wood filler</a> covers which fillers stay put outdoors, though on walking surfaces replacing a badly split board is the safer fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing new pressure-treated boards right away</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh treated lumber is saturated from the treatment process, and a sealant applied over that moisture cannot soak in. It either sits on top and turns tacky or fails within months. Wait until water dropped on the boards absorbs within a few minutes, which can take weeks or months depending on climate and lumber type.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a clear finish to stop graying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear sealants are water repellents first. Without pigment they let UV break down the wood surface, so cedar and redwood still drift toward gray. If keeping the original color is the priority, choose a lightly tinted or semi-transparent product and accept a slight color shift in exchange for much better sun protection.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blasting the deck with high pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pressure washer held close with a narrow tip gouges soft wood, raises the grain and leaves a fuzzy surface that soaks up finish unevenly. Use a cleaner to do the chemical work, keep pressure low, hold the wand back and follow the grain. On cedar, a garden hose and scrub brush is often enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating while the old coat still beads water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Applying fresh sealant over a finish that still repels water leaves the new coat sitting on the surface, where it dries sticky and collects dirt. Always do a water drop test first. Only recoat boards that absorb water, and wipe off anything the wood does not take in within the label's wait time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping railings and stairs in the estimate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pickets, rails, treads, risers and stringers can add half again to the square footage of a deck floor. Running out partway through forces a stop in the middle of a section, which leaves visible lap lines. Measure every surface, use the rough-wood coverage figure for older boards and buy an extra gallon.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealant versus stain: which one does what?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A deck sealant is mainly a water repellent and is usually clear or very lightly tinted, so the wood keeps its natural look. A stain contains pigment, which adds color and blocks UV light much more effectively. Many products are sold as stain and sealer in one and sit between the two. The more pigment a finish carries, the longer it generally lasts in sun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a clear deck sealant last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On an open, sunny deck most clear sealants keep beading water for about one to two years on the floor boards, and somewhat longer on railings and vertical surfaces. Shade, climate and foot traffic shift that range a lot. Lightly tinted versions of the same products typically last longer. The water drop test is the best way to know when your deck needs another coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can sealant go on top of an old stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can apply a penetrating sealant over an old penetrating stain once it has worn enough that water soaks in, after cleaning the deck. You cannot put a penetrating sealant over solid stain, paint or a peeling film, because it has nothing to soak into. Those finishes need stripping first, or a product designed to go over them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based or water-based deck sealant better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither wins outright. Oil-based penetrating sealers tend to soak deeper, warm the wood's color and fade gradually, which makes recoating easy. Water-based sealers dry faster, clean up with soap and water, have less odor and are legal in low-VOC states. Choose oil for dense or weathered wood in moderate weather, and water-based for short weather windows or tighter VOC rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal a deck in the fall or spring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Either works if the weather fits the label. Most products want air and surface temperatures between roughly 50 and 90 degrees, dry wood, and a rain-free day or two afterward. Fall often offers stable dry weather and cooler sun, which helps penetration. Spring sealing protects the deck before summer heat. Avoid days when dew or frost will settle before the finish dries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do composite decks need sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Composite and PVC decking are not designed to absorb a wood sealant, and most manufacturers advise against coating the boards. Applying one can leave a slippery or blotchy film and may affect the warranty. Clean composite decks with a cleaner the board maker approves. Any wood framing underneath, such as stair stringers or fascia, can still be treated.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of deck sealant should I apply?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most penetrating sealants call for one coat, and some ask for a second coat applied wet on wet before the first dries. Extra coats beyond what the wood can absorb only sit on the surface and turn tacky. Very dense hardwoods may only take one thin coat, while rough or weathered pine may take more. Always follow the product label.
              </p>
