          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most decks, TWP 1500 or Ready Seal's lightest tint is the sealant to buy: both penetrate, fade evenly and recoat with a cleaning instead of a stripper. DEFY Extreme Clear is the water-based alternative for low-VOC states or short weather windows, and Thompson's is a reasonable budget repellent if yearly recoats do not bother you.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you choose, the deck will look as good as the prep and timing allow. Clean and brighten, let the wood dry, back-brush every section, and recoat when water stops beading rather than on a fixed date. If you want more than a year or two between projects, step up to a tinted finish from our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a>.
